Gives strerror, perror and %m the words glibc gives each error number on
Linux, with no "(ENOENT)" after them: "Permission denied", not "Access
denied (EACCESS)", "File exists", not "File exists already (EEXIST)",
so that every program's messages read as they do on Linux and the
scripts that match them work. The texts are the host glibc 2.39's, read
with strerror; numbers Linux leaves unused, and any other, are "Unknown
error N" as there, and the XSI strerror_r fails with EINVAL for them, as
glibc's does. Meant for upstream, for the Linux ABI.

